<p>Japan&#x27;s Cabinet approved new guidelines that lift a longstanding ban on exporting lethal weapons, opening the way for overseas sales of items such as next-generation fighter jets and combat drones. The decision marks a clear shift in Japan&#x27;s postwar security policy as the government tries to strengthen the country&#x27;s domestic arms industry and deepen defense cooperation with partners. The move also reflects Tokyo&#x27;s view that the regional security environment has become more challenging, with concerns focused on China and uncertainty about future US commitments. Supporters see the change as a practical update that will help Japan work more closely with allies and share defense burdens, but it also breaks with decades of restrictions rooted in the country&#x27;s pacifist identity after World War II.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/U8BNJZboEwhmL3I5RrPtNfHvTXj9DxOss8gSDEj7_brTcYSS5AxpHz71kKrQsgObTarMepDbxd7Legg_5IMSkV44Uonrvsak9CHk7REYuGqStVvwP-liSe-TcodW8Isv-Gv9H0ivDVggWVP83zebtP1RmTAHnGEMaXK6hxo6NYb0J3k_k8XV7idCoBlPMMZpnScaq7GvQISbnA' alt='A Japanese marine unit takes part in exercises in Tokunoshima, Japan, in 2023.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Final hurdle: Several reports say the Cabinet decision cleared the last major procedural barrier in Japan&#x27;s postwar arms sales framework.</li><li>Likely products: Articles point to next-generation fighter jets and combat drones as examples of the systems that could be sold abroad under the new rules.</li><li>Industrial push: Tokyo tied the policy change to its effort to build up Japan&#x27;s defense manufacturing base as the government also raises military spending.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>The government: Coverage presents the rule change as part of a broader push to expand defense capacity, support the arms industry, and work more closely with security partners. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/japan-ends-decades-old-ban-on-lethal-arms-exports-in-major-policy-shift/a-76874734'>Deutsche Welle</a>)</li><li>Security-focused policymakers: The move is tied to a tougher regional environment, especially concerns about China and uncertainty about future US support. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/20/world/asia/japan-weapons-arms-sale-nato.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Critics of Japan&#x27;s postwar shift: Coverage describes the decision as a break with the pacifist principles that shaped Japan&#x27;s postwar identity, raising concern about how far the country is moving from its earlier self-imposed limits. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/21/japan-lifts-ban-on-lethal-weapons-exports-in-major-shift-of-pacifist-policy'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/21/japan-lifts-ban-on-lethal-weapons-exports-in-major-shift-of-pacifist-policy'>Japan lifts ban on lethal weapons exports in major shift of pacifist policy</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/04/21/g-s1-118178/japan-ban-lethal-weapons-exports'>Japan approves scrapping a ban on lethal weapons exports</a> - npr.org</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/japan-approves-scrapping-ban-lethal-weapons-exports-change-132224315'>Japan scraps a ban on lethal weapons exports in a change of its postwar pacifist policy</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/20/world/asia/japan-weapons-arms-sale-nato.html'>Japan to Sell More Weapons Abroad, Breaking With Postwar Pacifism</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/japan-ends-decades-old-ban-on-lethal-arms-exports-in-major-policy-shift/a-76874734'>Japan ends decades-old ban on lethal arms exports in major policy shift</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/japan-approves-scrapping-a-ban-on-lethal-weapons-exports-in-a-change-of-its-postwar-pacifist-policy/article70887084.ece'>Japan approves scrapping a ban on lethal weapons exports in a change of its postwar pacifist policy</a> - thehindu.com</li></ul>